<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Langres</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Andematunnum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Langres is a hilltop town in the Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on a plateau near the sources of the Marne. As Andematunnum it was the chief town of the Gallic Lingones and then a Roman city, and from late antiquity it was the seat of a bishopric whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were also dukes and peers of France. Its ramparts are among the most complete in France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/langres.jpg" alt="Langres" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vassil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Langres_121008_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Robert’s descendants ruled from Dijon until 1361, and the duchy kept its lasting core between the Saône, the Morvan and the plateau of <b>Langres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Diderot, the son of a cutler from <b>Langres</b>, was imprisoned at Vincennes in 1749 for his writings and gave a quarter of a century to the Encyclopédie.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Their chalk plains were rich farmland on the route between Reims and <b>Langres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/catalauni">Catalauni</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lingones held the uplands around <b>Andematunnum</b> (Langres), between the Sequani, the Aedui and the Leuci, astride the watershed between the Seine and Saône basins.</blockquote><a href="/state/lingones">Lingones</a></li><li><blockquote>Ellwangen abbey was founded around 764 by Hariolf, later bishop of <b>Langres</b>, and received Carolingian protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-provostry-of-ellwangen">Prince-Provostry of Ellwangen</a></li></ul></section>
